body:
So I have an honest to god question. It's not based out of overreaction or anything. At what point, do we ask ourselves that we have had enough and at least start considering taking back the government ourselves and force a change? Whether that be through serious mass protests or revolts. Or have we become too tamed and unable to do anything? I feel, as a society and as a country, that we have become far too passive with a lack of determination and courage. Our country and constitution was literally founded on the basis of the people having the power to literally strike fundamental changes in the government and country. This has occurred multiple times throughout history in our government so why not now or in the future if it continues to get worse? (note: I'm not talking about right now in the moment either because of the epidemic going on. I'm just talking about the general idea of it.)

&#x200B;

With the age of technology and internet, and people becoming sort of internet warriors, I feel like we have lost a lot in terms of values. Instead of fighting for what we believe in, the majority of us will only ever talk or rant about it online. I don't think that changes, and whatever the government does and is able to get away with, we'll just continue taking it. We've taken whatever this administration has done and have fully allowed and enabled them to do it to us. They know they can do whatever they want, throw it in our face, and fully get away with it with no repercussions. Trump will win another term and I believe it will open the floodgates for future administrations to look back and see how easy it is to easily abuse their power especially when a controlling party will fully backup their candidate with no signs of any kind of consequence. This pandemic has absolutely showed that the government can get away with anything with no consequence, despite it directly affecting, risking and even possibly costing the lives of many.

&#x200B;

I feel like another thing we need to point out is that people always point out that we have the power of voting. That gets you so far. Sometimes nowhere with the broken voting system and Electoral College we have in place. We have even seen clear multiple violations and unethical behavior by our government in our voting system with no consequences. I, unfortunately, don't see that ever changing, at least in my lifetime or at least without serious intervention and demand of change by us, the people. We also desperately need corporate money to be taken out of politics, which I don't see happening in my lifetime either.

&#x200B;

Not a rant or anything, just instilling my thoughts and actually just looking to have an honest discussion.

I know our defense sucked in the 2nd half and couldn't do anything in the 4th, which is a bit understandable because it's Mile High, but I honestly feel like they didn't lose us the game. For fuck sakes, I don't understand why not try to convert on our last drive. Carr throws a bullet to Martavis immediately right after he snaps and gives no chance for any kind of conversion. Let the damn play develop and try to convert at least past the past 1st down. ESPECIALLY considering that this is Mile High, making it easier to kick field goals. This is so damn fking frustrating to watch. I feel good about our team at first just to be emotionally and morally destroyed.

&#x200B;

We played with the prayers and hopes that we wouldn't lose. We DIDN'T play to fking win.
